  nonce: '6109'  # 6109
  message: "DONE (PR in pr_ref). Row 7 of the owner-authorised sequence. ADR-0057 §14.\n\nWHERE IT LANDED, AND WHY NOT IN THE MERGE PASS. finalize sub-step 7a, `_finalize_demote_superseded_stubs` → `demote_superseded_stubs(symbols, edges)`, registered right after `_finalize_edge_resolution` (7): `is_resolved` is the ADR-0037 verdict settled in Phase E, so the merge pass (Phase C) cannot know which edge is the stub. The row allowed either placement for the rank adjustment and required the stamp; both live here.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THE ROW DEMANDED CHANGED THE NUMBER. The row's 33 was an upper bound on (src, line) alone. On the committed aardvark-dns fixture (after the merge pass and dedup, with the resolution verdict imitated as finalize does it — a dst that is a real node is resolved, else a stub): 244 tree-sitter call edges end at an external stub; 63 share a (src, line) with some resolved edge; 27 share it with a resolved edge of the same type; only **3** also name the same callee — every one a `wrap` the syntactic arm left external and the SCIP arm resolved to `Result::wrap` (the `impl AardvarkWrap for Result` trait method rust.py cannot resolve). The other 24 same-site-same-type hits name a DIFFERENT callee: two calls on one line (`a.b(c.d())`); demoting them would have been the false supersession the row warned about. And 5 of the 27 were superseded only by a tree-sitter edge — the same producer's two calls on one line, not a contradiction. So the rule as landed requires ALL of: same src, a shared call line (call_lines-aware, not just `line`), the same edge_type, the same declared callee name key (the language's incumbent anchor name_key applied to both the resolved dst's name and the stub's dst_ref.name / id name slot), and a producer in the resolved edge's origin that the stub's origin lacks. Languages with a single anchored producer are untouched by construction (merge_participants).\n\nWHAT THE DEMOTION IS. rank_score *= SUPERSEDED_STUB_RANK_FACTOR (0.5, declared in finalize.py — the ADR-0039 ruling-3 multiplicative shape the type-hierarchy fan-out uses; joins WI-hukuf's table); confidence untouched; nothing deleted; edge count unchanged. Stamp: meta[\"superseded_by\"] = the resolved edge's id, meta[\"superseded_by_origin\"] = its origin — two new registered edge-meta keys (axis_meta_keys, docs/schema.json regenerated).\n\nEVIDENCE. Fixture (test_recorded_fixtures.py::TestSameSiteSupersessionOnBothArms): 244 stub calls, 3 demoted, each stamped with a resolved SCIP-only `calls` edge to `Result::wrap`, 24 co-located same-type sites untouched, every other rank_score unchanged, edge count unchanged (all pinned in AARDVARK_DNS_CALL_SITE_TALLY). Unit (test_finalize_superseded_stubs.py, 12 tests): the rule fires for another producer's resolved call and for a merged two-producer edge, honours call_lines; does NOT fire for a different callee, the same producer, a different line, a different edge type, two stubs, a single-producer language; reads the callee from the id when dst_ref is absent; takes rank_score from confidence when unset. Mutation: dropping the callee-name condition turns the unit and fixture pins red. Pipeline test exercises the sub-step through finalize (no demotion on the Python fixture).\n\nNOT ASSERTED. The factor 0.5 is a declared level, not measured; whether the 3 `Result::wrap` stubs should be REMOVED rather than demoted is exactly the coexist/replace question §14 answers \"demote\"; INV-fahub-class leverage on a real corpus is unmeasured until WI-dajif's instrument reports supersessions."  # 6109
